teachers in England Wells are going to  go on strike wave of winter strike  action Julian Keegan is the education  secretary with me this morning good  morning and good morning kids on the  program  um how could you avoid these strikes  well we've been doing what we can when I  very first started the job on the 25th  of October I had um you know the first  day I had a letter from all four unions  and it basically said we need an extra  two billion pounds next year and the  year after to pay for these uh increases  in pay and also the cost of living to  help schools with the cost of living so  that was the first challenge I was given  and we did secure that in the Autumn  statement so it's deeply disappointing  actually that one of the unions is only  one has um you know just scraped over  the line to get  um you know I think under half the  teachers voted for it but about 50 cents  53 turnout 90 percent voted for it so I  think it's just under half that actually  voted to strike in one Union in one  Union Yes and it will impact on many of  our children did pay come up in your  last meeting when you met with the Neu  because yeah I had the leader the  general secretary and she said that  you'd never even talked about pair when  she came in to see you we talked about  pay we talked about mostly in relation  to the pay review body of next year so  what is happening at the moment is the  submissions that we all put in and  everyone puts a submission into unions  the employers the government they take  lots of other inputs so in terms of  recruitment retention what's happening  in other sectors Etc and then they look  at all of that we've got to have that in  by the 27th of January so that's what  we're all working on to make sure that  we can see if we can try and come closer  together when we look at pay for the  next pay review body but Mary barstad  said and this is what she said we talked  about that education was very important  that teachers are very important and we  talked a bit about inflation and we  counted that wage Rises are causing  inflation but no we didn't talk about  money and we didn't talk about money  either for this year or next year well  what happened is we had a sort of wider  discussion and then the officials who  were going to go through all the line by  line submissions for the 27th followed  up with a meeting on the Friday then  we've got another meeting this week on  Wednesday and there's another meeting  with officials all day did you talk  about money with it we talked about it  in general but we didn't talk we didn't  go through line by line of the  submission because they were doing they  were starting to do that with the  officials but we talked about you know  the the principles of what the do you  know I think it would have been  important to talk about money and  potentially you could have fended off  this strike well you know what we've  done in terms of money is get the budget  to pay for the independent pay review  bodies and one thing that's important  when you look at because there's a lot  of information on pay so there's a five  percent rise for experienced teachers  and 8.9 rise for new teachers that's on  the bands the way a lot of public sector  pay works is you then get progression  within the bands so if you actually look  at almost every newly qualified teacher  and about 40 of experienced teachers  will actually be getting up to 15.9  because it will also include the  progression award as well they get  either for promotion or for progression  over time and then of course you've got  other benefits as well so it is  um you know an offer that the  independent pay-per-view body has put  together it's a lot higher than we put  in it's a lot higher than schools  budgeted but you're an education  secretary the unions come in to see you  why don't you talk about money well we  didn't talk about money but they're  saying that you didn't  well they also said I only met with them  for 45 minutes I was with them an hour  and 10 minutes um but but we did talk  about money but we did it wrong to say  that we didn't negotiate the pay that's  not what we're there to do we didn't  negotiate pay but what we did do was  talk about some of the challenges uh  workload challenges as well we talked  about and we we've gone through for next  year and we will by now they will have  gone through  um but there's a I think it's a six hour  meeting that's scheduled on Friday to go  through line by line so she's being  disingenuous to say that she came out  and you wouldn't talk about money  um I mean you know we talked about money  we talked about workload actually the  number one reason teachers leave  um and the retention rates are actually  the second lowest actually the numbers  for last year since 2010 at the rate  level but the the you know the number  one reason teachers leave is workload  and flexibility they want more  flexibility job shares being able to do  part-time that kind of thing so so  they're the things that we really need  to focus on teachers are doing a  brilliant job they're helping our kids  catch up from the pandemic obviously  they've done delivered now about three  million catch-up lessons and tutoring  and they're doing the most amazing job  but we need children in school now  luckily obviously the head teachers  unions  um one of the teachers unions and the  teaching assistant unions have not voted  for strike action so it's only one of  the teachers unions that has and we  issue guidance last night to head  teachers to try and uh well hopefully we  can keep as many schools open as  possible  I mean almost what's the message to a  mom on a zero hours contract who has to  take time off work because she can't  send her child to school because it's  shot it's deeply disappointing I am very  disappointed it's you know I'm  disappointed in the one Union that has  decided to go on strike our children  don't deserve it to be honest we do need  to keep teachers and children in school  and I will work with you know you don't  need to strike to get my attention I  will meet with people I'll be as  constructive as possible but we do need  to be fair and and the the number one  thing we need to do is tattle inflation  I've said that to you before there's no  point what we need to do is halve  inflation that's obviously what what uh  we've said we'll do  um and go be further Beyond but you know  more than that okay talk to me about the  gender reform uh Bill Nicholas sturgeon  said the decision by Richardson actor  blocked Scotland's gender Reform Bill is  a full frontal attack on our  democratically elected Scottish  Parliament and its ability to make its  own decisions on devolved matters what  do you think  um well I wouldn't agree with that I  mean the Scotland act deliberately was  set up with this power in it and I think  it was set up about 25 years ago  anticipating that there could be times  when legislation uh when it looked a bit  messy or there was legislation that  could potentially be competing and it  was set up I mean it was legislation  written by a labor government voted for  by the SMP so everybody agreed on the  the Scotland Act and the structure of it  and the fact that there would be this  power and when it was to be used and  obviously what we can't have is um you  know a lack of clarity in law in terms  of you know gender gender recognition  having two sort of schemes in place  across Great Britain so that's but I  don't understand those comments because  it was something they voted for it was  deliberately designed in the Scotland  act to help us navigate whether where  any of these sort of difficulties or  things that were less clear would you be  content for children in your schools at  16 to be able to say that they wanted to  change their gender and be able to do  that we have to be very sensitive to  Children you know it's not it's not we  are actually going to publish some  guidance and consult because it is a  very tricky area to get right to get it  has to be age-appropriate and but  children have to be supported as well so  I think what would you think 16 is too  young  um well I mean the the key thing will be  to have that discussion through the  consultation but what do you think  um no I don't actually I think 16 you  know I was working at 16. I was paying  tax at 16. I was could make decisions  for myself at 16.  um but you know it's not really about  what I think it's how we make sure we  get that right balance of supporting  children but also making sure that what  they're getting taught in schools is age  appropriate  um I'm sure you've got a view on this  how can we have faith in our police  force when one of them has just admitted  to 49 charges including 24 rapes it's  unbelievable it's really shocking and  appalling case and and obviously the  first thoughts are to the victims many  victims and their families  um and yeah it's really deeply shocking  actually what should they might be doing  about it well I think they are they I  mean obviously the matters come out and  they've apologized they've said they've  done a lot in terms of their vetting  procedures there's more they're doing I  think they've referred themselves to  this case to the independent police  organization as well but clearly they  they've got some work to do they do it's  really really important that we have  that trust in our police and of course  you know High sort of profile cases like  this or Wayne cousins does damage that  and so I think the the police have more  to do and the Met police have said that  they're going to be doing that okay um  it's good to talk to you thanks very  much today for taking the time thank you  good morning thank you

